(a) Permit required. It is unlawful for any person or business to operate a sidewalk café within the city without having first obtained a permit in accordance with this article.
(b) Fee. The fee for a permit issued under this article is one hundred dollars ($100.00) per calendar year which will not be prorated based upon the day of the year that the permit is issued. Once a permit has been issued, however, no portion of the permit fee will be refunded if the permit is revoked, suspended, or surrendered.
(c) Application process. Prior to operating a sidewalk café, a restaurant must apply for a permit from the city clerk.
(1) The application must contain the following information:
a. The name of the restaurant;
b. The name, address, phone number, and email of the owner of the restaurant;
c. The proposed location for the sidewalk café;
d. A description of the proposed use or occupancy of the public right-of-way;
e. Such other information as may be reasonably required by the city clerk.
(2) In conjunction with the application for a permit under this article, the applicant must submit a certified check for the full amount of the permit fee as well as a separate check or cash payment in the amount of twenty-five dollars ($25.00) to defray the administrative costs associated with processing the application. If the application is denied or refused, or if the applicant withdraws its application prior to the permit being issued, the permit fee will be refunded but the twenty-five dollars ($25.00) administrative fee will be retained by the city.
(3) In conjunction with the permit application, the applicant must also submit proof of the liability insurance required for the operation of a sidewalk café under this article before a permit will be issued. For the purpose of compliance with this section, an original certificate of insurance will suffice as evidence that the insurance requirement has been met prior to the issuance of a permit.
(4) The application for a sidewalk café permit will be issued by the city clerk only after review and approval by the city's codes department.
(5) The following criteria will be considered to determine whether a permit may be granted or renewed:
a. Compliance by the applicant or permit holder with the provisions of this article and all other relevant laws or ordinances of the city;
b. Payment record and status of business licenses, alcoholic beverage license fees, taxes, or other monies owed by the applicant or permit holder;
c. The character and reputation of the applicant or permit holder;
d. The sidewalk café's actual or proposed affect on the primary purpose and use of the public right-of-way, including, but not limited to, the efficiency and clearance of the areas reserved for unobstructed pedestrian access; on-street parking; vehicular travel lanes; loading and unloading areas; hydrants and utility access; crosswalks and accessibility ramps; driveways and alleys; transit or bus service stops; and intersection visibility;
e. The sidewalk café's actual or proposed affect on the right-of-way area; sidewalk dimensions, materials, and existing conditions; obstacles and obstructions; street signs; streetscape elements such as trees, lights, benches, trash cans, sign posts, etc.; and proximity of other sidewalk cafés;
f. The sidewalk café's actual or proposed affect on nearby properties, including adjoining property owners; and
g. All other relevant issues related to the public's health, safety, and welfare.
(6) A sidewalk café permit is valid for one (1) year from January 1 through December 31. An existing permit may be renewed by filing an application for renewal on or before October 31 of the year for which the permit was issued.
(7) Permits issued under this Article may not be transferred from one (1) permit holder to another or from one (1) location to another, and any change in ownership of any permitted entity other than a publicly traded corporation will necessitate a new application and full payment of the administrative and permit fees.
(8) An applicant may appeal the denial of a permit under this article by providing written notification to the city clerk at city hall within ten (10) calendar days of the denial of the permit application. The written notification must include the factual and legal basis upon which the applicant's appeal is based. The appeal will entitle the permit applicant to an administrative hearing before the city council.
